            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x^2+\\frac1{x^2}=6",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", which is rational.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p22-q68-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Rationalise the reciprocal, square both expressions and add.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=(1-\\sqrt2)^2\\\\x^2&=3-2\\sqrt2\\\\\\frac1x&=\\frac{1+\\sqrt2}{(1-\\sqrt2)(1+\\sqrt2)}\\\\\\frac1x&=-1-\\sqrt2\\\\\\frac1{x^2}&=(-1-\\sqrt2)^2\\\\\\frac1{x^2}&=3+2\\sqrt2\\\\x^2+\\frac1{x^2}&=(3-2\\sqrt2)+(3+2\\sqrt2)\\\\x^2+\\frac1{x^2}&=6\\end{align*}$$\nSince 6 is an integer, the sum is rational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
